1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugene_Maurice,_Count_of_Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugene Maurice of Savoy (French: Eugène Maurice de Savoie; March 2, 1635 – June 6, 1673) was count of Soissons, a French general and father of Prince Eugene of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugene Maurice was born in Chambéry, Savoy. He was son of Thomas Francis, Prince of Carignano and Marie de Bourbon, Countess of Soissons. He was grandson of Charles Emmanuel I, Duke of Savoy and Catherine Micaela of Austria, daugh
2 CONC ter of Philip II of Spain and Elisabeth of France, herself daughter of King Henry II and Catherine de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married on February 21, 1657 Olimpia Mancini, a niece of cardinal Mazarin, daughter of Michele Mancini and Geronima Mazarini.
2 CONT
2 CONT He obtained high military posts through his wife's influence. He played a role in defeating the Spaniards at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Dunes_(1658) ''battle of the Dunes''] in 1658. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He also took part in the campaigns at Flanders (1667), Franche-Comté (1668) and Holland (1672), and was present as ambassador extraordinary of France at the coronation of Charles II of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at Unna in Westphalia in 1673, out of a deadly fever (although there were voices that he had been poisoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT #.Louis Thomas, Count of Soissons (1657–1702) married Urania de La Cropte and had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT #Philippe, "Abbot of Soissons" (1659–1693) unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Louis Jules, Cavaliere of Savoy (1660–1683) killed at the battle of Petronell against the Turks known as the Cavaliere di Savoia
2 CONT
2 CONT #Emanuel Philibert, Count of Dreux (1662–1676) unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Prince Eugene of Savoy (1663–1736) famous general.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Princess Marie Jeanne of Savoy (1665-1705) Mademoiselle de Soissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Princess Louise Philiberte of Savoy (1667–1726) Mademoiselle de Druex.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Princess Françoise of Savoy (1668–1671), Mademoiselle de Dreux.
