1 NOTE He was educated privately and at the École Militaire. Before his accession 
2 CONT to the throne he bore the title count of Flanders. His democratic manner 
2 CONT made him the most popular member of the reigning house. He traveled widely 
2 CONT and was a student of politics and economics. In 1898 and again in 1919 he 
2 CONT visited the U.S. In 1900 he made an extended tour of the Belgian Congo and 
2 CONT on his return to Belgium urged the need of railroad development and of 
2 CONT reform in the treatment of the Congolese; when he became king, he ordered 
2 CONT many improvements in the administration of this colony. On the death of 
2 CONT Leopold II in 1909, Albert acceded to the throne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT While on a visit to Berlin in 1913, Albert was informed of Germany's plans 
2 CONT for war by Emperor William II. He immediately warned France and on July 
2 CONT 31, 1914, sent a personal letter to the German emperor informing him that 
2 CONT Belgium would remain neutral. When the letter was ignored and German 
2 CONT troops poured into Belgium, Albert assumed active command of his army and 
2 CONT directed a successful delaying action against invasion. He remained with 
2 CONT his troops throughout the war. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After World War I he played an active role in the reconstruction of his 
2 CONT country and in 1919 made a plea to the Allies for the abolition of the 
2 CONT Treaty of London, which made Belgium neutral ground and thus vulnerable to 
2 CONT invasion. As a result, the abolition of the treaty was incorporated into 
2 CONT the Treaty of Versailles. Albert supported general industrial expansion 
2 CONT and the development of a strong merchant fleet as the best methods of 
2 CONT national recovery. He was succeeded by his eldest son, Leopold III.
