1 NOTE was an English writer, now known for her ghost stories and diaries. She also wrote novels and edited a number of anthologies, as well as writing for children and on the British Royal family.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913 she met D. H. Lawrence in Margate, and became a friend and correspondent.[1] During World War I, to supplement her husband's income, she took a position as secretary to Peter Pan creator J. M. Barrie, with whom she became 
2 CONC close friends, continuing to work for him until his death in 1937. Barrie left the bulk of his estate – minus the Peter Pan works – to her.[2] The author L. P. Hartley became a lifelong friend after they met in the early 1920s.
