1 TITL Hon.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Stephen James Napier Tennant (21 April 1906 - 28 February 1987) was a British aristocrat known for his decadent lifestyle. It is said, albeit apocryphally, that he spent most of his life in bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in England, the youngest son of a Scots peer, Lord Glenconner, and the former Pamela Wyndham, one of The Souls. His mother was also a cousin of Lord Alfred Douglas (1870-1945), Oscar Wilde's lover and a sonneteer. On h
2 CONC is father's death, Tennant's mother married Lord Grey, a fellow bird-lover. Tennant's eldest brother was Edward - "Bim" - who was killed in the First World War.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the twenties and thirties, Tennant was an important member - the "Brightest", it is said - of the "Bright Young People." His friends included Rex Whistler, Cecil Beaton, the Sitwells, Lady Diana Manners and the Mitford girl
2 CONC s part of the set that made the Nordstrom Sisters popular at The Ritz in 1939. He is widely considered to be the model for Cedric Hampton in Nancy Mitford's novel ''Love in a Cold Climate''; one of the inspirations for Lord Sebast
2 CONC ian Flyte in Waugh's ''Brideshead Revisited'', and a model for Hon. Miles Malpractice in some of his other novels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also during the 1920s and 1930s, Tennant was the lover of the poet Siegfried Sassoon.[1] Prior to this he had proposed to a friend, Elizabeth Lowndes, but had been rejected. (Hoare relates how Tennant discussed plans with Lowndes 
2 CONC about bringing his Nanny with them on their honeymoon.) His relationship with Sassoon, however, was to be his most important: it lasted some four years before Tennant off-handedly put an abrupt end to it. Sassoon was reportedly de
2 CONC pressed afterwards for three months, until Sassoon married in 1933 and became a father in 1936. It was common for Gay men to marry a women in the era before Human Rights protections, when homosexuals were criminalized for their or
2 CONC ientation, often imprisoned (as happened to Oscar Wilde), committed to insane asylums, tortured, lobotomized, electro-shock "treated" (which often involved laying in a full bathtub with live electric wires placed in the water), an
2 CONC d even executed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For most of his life, Tennant tried to start or finish a novel - ''Lascar''. It is popularly believed that he spent the last 17 years of his life in bed at his family manor at Wilsford, Wiltshire, which he had redecorated by Syrie
2 CONC  Maugham. Though undoubtedly idle, he was not truly lethargic: he made several visits to the United States and Italy, and struck up many new friendships, despite his later reputation as a recluse. This became increasingly true onl
2 CONC y towards the last years of his life. Yet even then, his life was not uneventful: he became landlord to V. S. Naipaul who immortalized Tennant in his novel ''The Enigma of Arrival''. When Tennant died in 1987, he had far outlived 
2 CONC most of his contemporaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
