1 NAME Joan Olivia /Wyndham/
2 GIVN Joan Olivia
2 SURN Wyndham
2 _MARNM Rowdon
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 OCT 1921
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 APR 2007
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joan Wyndham Author whose charmingly louche wartime diaries brought her late fame Kathryn Hughes Monday 16 April 2007 23.49 BST
2 CONT  View more sharing options Shares 1
2 CONT On Friday February 2 1940, Joan Wyndham, who has died aged 85, wrote: "Went to Confession at the Servite - Father Carato, again, three Hail Marys. While I was kneeling in front of the altar saying my penance, I suddenly thought of
2 CONC  G kissing me and a kind of delicious stabbing pain ran through my stomach, so sharp and strong that I winced." Such was a typical entry in Wyndham's wartime teenage diaries. First published as Love Lessons (1985), with a sequel L
2 CONC ove is Blue (1987) they turned Wyndham into a late-flowering author. Shockingly candid, furnished with moodily seductive painters, crush-worthy WAAF officers and cheap booze, Wyndham's account of life as an upper-class gel despera
2 CONC te to lose her virginity while the bombs fell overhead became a critical and commercial success. Within a few months she was propelled from a position on the edge of the London bohemian arts scene into something of a minor literar
2 CONC y celebrity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sign up to our Bookmarks newsletter
2 CONT  Read more Wyndham was born at Clouds, the Wiltshire family mansion built by the arts and crafts architect, Philip Webb. The fact that Clouds later became a rehabilitation facility for those struggling with alcohol and drug depend
2 CONC ency always struck the extremely bibulous Wyndham as profoundly funny. Her parents were the mismatched Richard "Dirty Dick" Wyndham, a scion of the aristocratic family of Petworth House, and Iris, the illegitimate daughter of Lord
2 CONC  French, viceroy of Ireland. The wedding night had not been a success, perhaps because the bride had been briefed by her own mother that the chief rule for marital happiness was "to always use lots of scent and never let him see y
2 CONC ou brush your teeth". The couple had split up by the time Joan was four. Iris now settled with her only child in a house off the Fulham Road, west London, which she shared with her lesbian best friend, the sculptor Sidonie "Sid" H
2 CONC ouselander. The household was intensely Roman Catholic and Joan continued to go to mass and confession throughout her teens.
2 CONT
2 CONT An early crush on the "pretty smashing" John Gielgud led Joan into a more general love affair with the theatre, and at the age of 16 she won a place at Rada. However the war put paid to any actorly ambitions and she turned instead
2 CONC  to art, enrolling at Chelsea Poly where she was taught sculpture by the "darling" Henry Moore. Taking studio space at nearby Redcliffe Gardens, Joan now immersed herself in the life of a shabby though upper-class art student. Day
2 CONC s in the studio spent painting from life models were interspersed with impromptu parties, unhappy love affairs and, eventually, the much-wanted loss of her virginity. As she explained later, the trigger had been a near-miss with a
2 CONC  German bomb, as a result of which she rationalised: "If I survive this, I should go round to Rupert's and get myself de-virginised." In the event, the de-virginising was a distinct disappointment, with the pragmatic Joan declarin
2 CONC g: "Goodness, is that all it is? I'd rather have a jolly good smoke and go to the pictures any day!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Joan joined the WAAF where she alternated between wearing lock-tight knickers to dissuade any casual lovers, including a boozed-up Dylan Thomas, and managing to conduct affairs with both Hugh Fraser, later a Tory MP and fir
2 CONC st husband of Lady Antonia Fraser, and a Norwegian lieutenant. In her demob years she racketed around a number of jobs, including setting up Oxford's first espresso bar, running a hippyish restaurant in Portobello Road, and cookin
2 CONC g for the Royal Court theatre. All these ways of earning a living played to Joan's strengths: gregarious, unshockable and without a shred of "side", she enjoyed nothing more than making other people feel happy, well fed and slight
2 CONC ly naughty.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was this trick of turning life into a party that made Joan such fun to be around. One man who lodged, in the 1980s, with her in the Fulham home she shared with her second husband Shura Shivarg remembers "a constant stream of ex
2 CONC traordinarily interesting visitors. You literally never knew who you would be sitting down to breakfast with."
2 CONT
2 CONT People arrived for the night, stayed for days and, in some cases, never left at all. Joan's particular preference was for handsome young men who could be counted upon to feed her beloved cats. Always avid to know what the younger 
2 CONC generation was thinking, in later life she became a ferocious Chelsea fan, insisting on having Sky television installed so that she would never miss a match.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was quite by chance that Wyndham came to be published. In the 1980s, her younger daughter, Camilla, found her wartime diaries in the attic and, enchanted by their mixture of naivety and loucheness, took them to the publishers W
2 CONC illiam Heinemann. Like her friend Quentin Crisp, Joan relished becoming famous in later life. She particularly enjoyed her own shock value, relating with glee how an appearance on Loose Ends had led to a barrage of complaints from
2 CONC  Radio 4 listeners.
2 CONT
2 CONT In her private life, too, she refused to behave. At the birthday party of a particularly grand relative, she was asked politely to move to one side because the Queen was approaching. "Oh fuck off!' said Joan loudly, who was becomi
2 CONC ng less mobile as she grew older, to which the monarch responded with a particularly frosty stare.
2 CONT
2 CONT In recent years Wyndham produced two further volumes of memoir, Anything Once (1992) and Dawn Chorus (1994). These, though, did not quite reach the same artless heights as the work she had written while still an anonymous teenager
2 CONC . However shortly before her death Wyndham had submitted an autobiographical novel to her agent, a hopeful sign for the many fans of her early writing. According to one literary editor of a national newspaper: "Joan's wartime diar
2 CONC ies deserve to be absolute classics, to stand shoulder-to-shoulder with Pepys."
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by her husband and their daughter Camilla, and Clare, her daughter from her first marriage to Maurice Rowden.
2 CONT
2 CONT · Joan Olivia Wyndham, writer, born October 11 1921; died April 8 2007
