0 @I6000000189255808831@ INDI
1 NAME Irene B /Burns/
2 GIVN Irene B
2 SURN Burns
2 _MARNM Williams-Parisi
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Kattelville Cemetery, Kattelville, Broome County, New York, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 AUG 1932
2 PLAC Colesville, Broome County, New York, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 NOV 2012
2 PLAC New York, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} On August 22nd 1932, Irene B. Burns was born in Colesville, NY. She peacefully passed away November 9th 2012.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene was the daughter of Genevia and Earl Burns. She had a total of 14 siblings, 4 sisters and 10 brothers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As was more common then, Irene went to live with the family of Marjorie and Joseph Villecco of Tunnel, NY to help with their children; Earl, Sandra and Joanie. She became a part of the Villecco family and resided with them until h
2 CONC er marriage.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene graduated from Harpursville Central School in June 1951 and went to work for the Binghamton Press. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene was married to John (Jack) D. Williams of Nimmonsburg, NY, on October 16, 1954. They had four children; Brenda, Timothy, Jon and Joni. At the age of 41, Jack passed away and Irene took up the reins of raising their four chil
2 CONC dren by herself. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene believed that hard work would get you anywhere you wanted to go in life and she was always striving to better herself. She worked at the medical center off Upper Front Street in Binghamton and then worked for Farm Credit for
2 CONC  many years. She also owned a coffee business and participated in several other small business ventures. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene was beautiful and always dressed to the nines. She was a smart, sharp woman who always wanted to present her best to the world. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a loving and protective mother who worked tirelessly to give her children better lives. But Irene also loved to have fun and was willing to try almost anything, including a move to Alaska in her early sixties. She loved he
2 CONC r family and friends, golf, hiking, good food and good wine, beautiful clothes and sporty cars. Irene was, as we like to say, "Jazzy." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene had six grandchildren; Brie, Parker, Samantha, Ashley, Chelsea and Caitlin. She left them with fond memories of eating ice cream for dinner and parties at Chuck E. Cheese's.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1998 she was diagnosed with Parkinson's disease. Irene carried on to live an active life as only those with boundless energy can. She continued to golf and volunteer for the "Make a Wish Foundation." 
2 CONT
2 CONT She moved to Cortland to a nursing care facility in 2006.  Her earthly body has departed. Her loving spirit and legacy are carried forward by her loving family: Brenda and Mike Hedges, Tim Williams, Jon Williams, Joni and Ron Hart
2 CONC mann, Brie Hoblin, Parker Hoblin, Samantha Williams, Ashley Williams, Chelsea Hartmann, Caitlin Hartmann, Earl Villecco, Sandy and Floyd Darrow, and Joanie Villecco, her three sisters; Beatrice Dickinson, Violet Knapp, Charlotte S
2 CONC uraw and five brothers; George, Charles, Ken, Jerry and Ray Burns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral Services will be held at the Osterhoudt- Madden Funeral Home 69-71 Maple Street Harpursville on Mon. at 4 p.m.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Private burial will be in Kattelville Cemetery at the convenience of the family.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The family will receive friends at the Osterhoudt-Madden Funeral Home on Mon. from 2 to 4 p.m. prior to service.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/120493167/irene-b-williams-parisi Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://
